      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Lanovsky compares people without documents to rats. He gets a phone call saying they found them in the fridge. He tells Viktor to come with him. Let us listen to their conversation in Russian first.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "В этом мире крысы — враги.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Их все очень ненавидят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "И вы тоже крыс не любите.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Мыши очень похожи на них.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, я их не люблю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Они как милые мыши.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Но крыс все ненавидят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Извините, мне звонят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Да, я вас слушаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Где они? В холодильнике?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Замечательно, мы сейчас идём.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Виктор Петрович, пойдёмте со мной.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "В этом мире крысы — враги.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In this world rats are enemies.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'that' straight to 'enemy'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Их все очень ненавидят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everyone hates them very much.' You'll hear 'them' all the time in the show, here connected with 'hate'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "И вы тоже крыс не любите.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And you also do not like rats.' The first content word means 'you', and 'to love' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Мыши очень похожи на них.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Mice are very similar to them.' You'll hear 'mouse' all the time in the show, here connected with 'them'. Chef Viktor answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, я их не люблю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, I do not like them.' The key word here means 'I', which Chef Viktor combines with 'them'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Они как милые мыши.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They are like cute mice.' You'll hear 'they' all the time in the show, here connected with 'mouse'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Но крыс все ненавидят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But everyone hates rats.' In Russian, 'rat' leads into 'hate' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Извините, мне звонят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Excuse me, they are calling me.' the speaker starts with 'sorry' and pairs it with 'to me' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Да, я вас слушаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, I am listening to you.' The first content word means 'I', and 'I'm listening' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Где они? В холодильнике?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where are they? In the fridge?' the speaker starts with 'where' and pairs it with 'they' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Замечательно, мы сейчас идём.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Great, we are going now.' Here 'wonderful' sets up the idea, while 'are going' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Виктор Петрович, пойдёмте со мной.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
